            <ArticleTitle>A Woman in Her 50s With a Mass in One Lung and a Cavitary Lesion in the Other.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e15-e19</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0012-3692(17)30227-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.chest.2017.01.038</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE PRESENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A woman in her 50s with no significant medical history presented with low-grade fever and cough of 3 days' duration, which was productive of blood-streaked sputum. She was an active smoker, with a 30 pack-year history of smoking. She denied chest pain, chronic cough, exertional dyspnea, or constitutional symptoms.</AbstractText>
